window.DL_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What shape does (8, 1, 6, 1) + (7, 1, 5) broadcast to?","o":["It raises an error","(8, 7, 6, 1)","(8, 7, 6, 5)","(8, 1, 6, 5)"],"a":2,"w":"Aligned on the right: 1 vs 5 → 5, 6 vs 1 → 6, 1 vs 7 → 7, and 8 against a padded 1 → 8."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Predictions have shape (N, 1) and targets shape (N,). What does ((pred − y) ** 2).mean() compute?","o":["The mean over an (N, N) matrix of every prediction minus every target","The correct mean squared error","A RuntimeError","The mean squared error of the first example only"],"a":0,"w":"It broadcasts to (N, N). Minimising it makes every prediction the mean of y; measured R² fell from 0.98 to 0.00."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does a.t().view(12) fail on a contiguous (3, 4) tensor?","o":["Transposed tensors cannot be reshaped at all","view only works on the GPU","12 is not the right number of elements","The transpose is a non-contiguous view, and no strides can describe a flat 12-vector over that memory order"],"a":3,"w":"Use reshape, which copies when a view is impossible, or call contiguous() first."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Logits have shape (128, 10). Which call gives each example a probability distribution over the 10 classes?","o":["logits.softmax(dim=0)","logits.softmax(dim=1)","logits.softmax(dim=0).t()","logits.sum(dim=1).softmax(dim=0)"],"a":1,"w":"dim names the axis that is normalised over: the classes. Over dim 0 the model in this chapter fell from 84.0% to 36.6%."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How much extra memory did expanding a (1, 4096) tensor to (4096, 4096) allocate, against repeat?","o":["64 MB each","32 MB for expand, 64 MB for repeat","0 MB for expand (stride 0), 64 MB for repeat","0 MB for both"],"a":2,"w":"expand is a view with stride 0 along the stretched axis; repeat copies the data 4,096 times."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"X is (64, 784), W is (784, 256) and G = ∂L/∂(XW) is (64, 256). What is ∂L/∂W?","o":["G Wᵀ","Xᵀ G","G Xᵀ","X G"],"a":1,"w":"Xᵀ (784, 64) times G (64, 256) is (784, 256), W’s shape. It also sums the 64 per-example contributions."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does every row of the softmax Jacobian sum to zero?","o":["Softmax is linear","The Jacobian is diagonal","Logits are centred to zero","Probabilities always sum to 1, so moving any logit only moves probability between classes"],"a":3,"w":"Measured for z = (2, 1, 0.1, −1): every row summed to 0 and J matched diag(p) − ppᵀ exactly."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does one backward pass of backprop compute, in Jacobian terms?","o":["A vector-Jacobian product: the loss gradient pushed back through each layer","The full Jacobian of the network","One column of the Jacobian per parameter","The Hessian"],"a":0,"w":"Reverse mode never builds a Jacobian; it multiplies a vector by each layer’s Jacobian implicitly."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A finite-difference gradient check in float32 gives relative error 10⁻³. What should you conclude?","o":["The gradient is certainly correct","The gradient is certainly wrong","Nothing yet: float32 could only reach about 10⁻⁴ here at best, so rerun in float64","Use a smaller h, such as 10⁻⁹"],"a":2,"w":"In float64 the measured error fell to 2.6 × 10⁻¹⁰; at h = 10⁻⁹ in float32 the estimate was exactly 0."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A bias b of shape (256,) is added to activations of shape (64, 256). What is ∂L/∂b?","o":["The upstream gradient G, shape (64, 256)","G summed over the batch axis, shape (256,)","G averaged over the 256 features","Zero, because biases do not affect the loss"],"a":1,"w":"b was broadcast over the batch in the forward pass, so its gradient sums over the batch in the backward pass."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does bf16 trade away to get fp32’s range in 16 bits?","o":["The sign bit","Exponent bits","Mantissa bits: it keeps only 7, about two significant digits","Nothing; it is strictly better than fp16"],"a":2,"w":"bf16 is 1 sign, 8 exponent and 7 mantissa bits; fp16 is 1, 5 and 10. Its epsilon is 7.8 × 10⁻³ against fp16’s 9.8 × 10⁻⁴."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You add 0.01 to an fp16 accumulator 100,000 times. What do you get?","o":["32: once the gap between fp16 numbers exceeds 0.02, each addition rounds back to the same value","1,000 exactly","inf","NaN"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: it was stuck at 32.0 by step 3,000. bf16 stuck at 4; torch.sum, which accumulates in fp32, gave 1,000."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does subtracting the maximum logit before exponentiating not change the softmax?","o":["It does change it slightly, but the error is small","Because the maximum logit is always zero","Because exp is linear","Because exp(z − m) / Σ exp(z − m) = exp(z) / Σ exp(z): the factor exp(−m) cancels"],"a":3,"w":"Softmax depends only on differences between logits, so the shift is free and keeps every exponent at most 0."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The formula E[x²] − E[x]² returned a variance of −8 in fp32. What happened?","o":["The data had negative values","Catastrophic cancellation: two numbers of about 10⁸, each rounded to the nearest 8, were subtracted to find a difference of 1","fp32 overflowed","The GPU has a bug"],"a":1,"w":"Centring first (what torch.var does) gave 0.9992, matching float64."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does loss scaling do in fp16 training?","o":["Clips large gradients","Lowers the learning rate","Multiplies the loss, and so every gradient, by a constant so small gradients stay above fp16’s underflow threshold; it is divided out before the step","Converts the loss to bf16"],"a":2,"w":"Scaling by 1,024 moved all but 0.001% of the measured gradients into fp16’s range. bf16 does not need it."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A module stores its hidden layers as self.layers = [nn.Linear(784, 256), nn.Linear(256, 256)] . What happens?","o":["PyTorch raises an error at construction","The layers run in forward but are not in parameters() or state_dict(), so they never train and are never saved","Everything works normally","The layers train but are not saved"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 2,570 of 269,322 parameters were visible; accuracy 75.8% vs 87.1%. Use nn.ModuleList."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does loss.backward() do to a parameter’s .grad?","o":["Replaces it with the new gradient","Sets it to zero","Applies the optimiser step","Adds the new gradient to whatever is already there"],"a":3,"w":"Gradients accumulate, which is why zero_grad() is needed each step (and why gradient accumulation works)."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a forward pass use 896 MB with autograd but 256 MB under torch.no_grad()?","o":["With autograd on, every operation keeps the tensors its backward pass will need","no_grad uses fp16","no_grad skips some layers","no_grad frees the weights"],"a":0,"w":"The arithmetic is identical (both took about 23 ms); only the saved activations differ."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You need to evaluate a model with dropout and batch norm. What do you call?","o":["Only model.eval()","Only torch.no_grad()","Both: model.eval() switches dropout and batch norm to inference behaviour, no_grad stops recording the graph","Neither; PyTorch detects evaluation automatically"],"a":2,"w":"They control different things; evaluation needs both."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Calling L.backward() a second time raises “Trying to backward through the graph a second time”. Why?","o":["Gradients can only be computed once per model","The first backward freed the tensors the graph had saved; pass retain_graph=True if you truly need a second pass","The loss became zero","The optimiser has not stepped yet"],"a":1,"w":"Freeing the graph as backward goes is what keeps training memory bounded."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On this Windows machine, why was the first epoch with 8 workers slower than with 0?","o":["Workers compete for the GPU","pin_memory was off","Each spawned worker starts a new Python process and imports PyTorch, about 1.4 s each, before producing a batch","Workers process images in a different order"],"a":2,"w":"From epoch 2, with persistent workers, 8 workers delivered 161,000 img/s against 34,000 with none."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The loader produces 18,000 img/s and the training step consumes 29,600 img/s. What happens?","o":["The GPU waits for data for roughly 40% of each step","The loader drops images to keep up","Training runs at 29,600 img/s","The batch size shrinks automatically"],"a":0,"w":"The slower stage sets the pace: 18,000/29,600 ≈ 61% busy."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Fashion-MNIST sorted by label, trained without shuffling: what did the model predict on the test set?","o":["Every class equally","Only class 0","The correct class 86% of the time","Class 8 or 9, the last classes it saw, for about 98% of images"],"a":3,"w":"Each run of batches pulled the weights towards one class; it ended fitting the end of the file. Accuracy 14.9%."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A medical dataset has several images per patient. How should you split it?","o":["Randomly by image, with a fixed seed","By patient, so all of a patient’s images land in the same split","By image, stratified by label","It does not matter as long as the test set is large"],"a":1,"w":"Otherwise the model can score well by recognising patients rather than reading images."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When does a custom collate_fn become necessary?","o":["Whenever num_workers is above 0","When labels are integers","When examples have different shapes, such as variable-length sequences, so the default stacking fails","Only for iterable-style datasets"],"a":2,"w":"The default collate stacks tensors of equal shape; padding or keeping lists needs your own function."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Minimising mean squared error is maximum likelihood under which assumption?","o":["Targets follow a Laplace distribution around the prediction","Targets are the prediction plus Gaussian noise of constant variance","Targets are categorical","There is no noise"],"a":1,"w":"The Gaussian log-density is −(y − f)²/2σ² plus terms that do not depend on the weights."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A new 10-class classifier’s first-batch loss is 2.31. What does that suggest?","o":["It has already learned a lot","The loss function is broken","It is predicting close to uniform probabilities, as an untrained model should: log 10 ≈ 2.303","The learning rate is too high"],"a":2,"w":"Measured on a fresh MLP: 2.312. A start far from log K points to a bug."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the gradient of cross-entropy with respect to the logits?","o":["softmax(z) − onehot(y)","z − y","−1 / p_y","softmax(z) · (1 − softmax(z))"],"a":0,"w":"Large when the model is confidently wrong, which is why it trains well; squared error on softmax lacks this."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why predict log σ² rather than σ² in a Gaussian NLL output?","o":["It is more accurate","PyTorch requires it","It makes the loss linear","Any real number is a valid log-variance, so the variance stays positive with no constraint"],"a":3,"w":"A raw variance output could reach zero or go negative and make the NLL infinite."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which identity links cross-entropy, entropy and KL divergence?","o":["H(p, q) = H(p) − KL(p ‖ q)","H(p, q) = H(p) + KL(p ‖ q)","KL(p ‖ q) = KL(q ‖ p)","H(p, q) = H(q)"],"a":1,"w":"Checked here: 1.4998 = 0.5003 + 0.9995. With one-hot targets H(p) = 0."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The GPU: Kernels, Memory & Throughput","f":"p7-the-gpu-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A timer around c = a @ b on the GPU reads 0.04 ms, without a synchronise. What did it measure?","o":["The matrix multiply","The copy to the GPU","Only the time to queue the kernel; the work happens later","The CPU fallback"],"a":2,"w":"With torch.cuda.synchronize() the same multiply measured 4.0 ms."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The GPU: Kernels, Memory & Throughput","f":"p7-the-gpu-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a 64 × 64 matrix multiply run faster on the CPU?","o":["It is too little work to fill the GPU, so the GPU’s fixed per-kernel overhead dominates","The CPU has more memory","GPUs cannot multiply small matrices","The GPU was in fp16"],"a":0,"w":"About 17 µs of GPU time whatever the size, against 4 µs on the CPU; the GPU won from n = 256."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The GPU: Kernels, Memory & Throughput","f":"p7-the-gpu-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does pinned host memory change?","o":["It stores tensors on the GPU","It compresses tensors","It makes GPU kernels faster","The OS cannot move it, so the GPU can copy from it directly and asynchronously: 52 vs 21 GB/s here"],"a":3,"w":"Pageable memory is first staged through a pinned buffer by the driver."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The GPU: Kernels, Memory & Throughput","f":"p7-the-gpu-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After freeing a 256 MB tensor, memory_allocated drops by 256 MB but nvidia-smi does not change. Why?","o":["The tensor was not really freed","PyTorch’s caching allocator keeps the memory reserved for reuse; empty_cache() returns it","nvidia-smi updates once a minute","The memory leaked"],"a":1,"w":"In the measured sequence the next 128 MB tensor was served from that cache with no new reservation."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The GPU: Kernels, Memory & Throughput","f":"p7-the-gpu-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A loop applies 1,000 additions to a 1,000-element GPU tensor and takes 8.6 ms. What limits it?","o":["Memory bandwidth","Arithmetic throughput","Launch overhead: about 8.6 µs per operation regardless of size","PCIe copies"],"a":2,"w":"Fusing the operations, or batching more data into each, is the fix."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Experiments: Seeds, Logging & Learning Curves","f":"p8-experiments-seeds-and-curves.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Two configurations differ by 0.04 points on average over 10 seeds, with a standard error of 0.16. What should you conclude?","o":["The second is better","There is no evidence of a difference","The first is better","Run one more seed to break the tie"],"a":1,"w":"t = 0.27. A single seed of each picked the “winner” 52% vs 47% of the time."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Experiments: Seeds, Logging & Learning Curves","f":"p8-experiments-seeds-and-curves.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You seed everything and train a CNN twice on the GPU. The weights differ slightly. Why?","o":["Seeding does not work on GPUs","The data loader ignored the seed","A hardware fault","Some GPU kernels sum in a different order each run, and floating-point addition is not associative"],"a":3,"w":"Measured: the MLP was bit-identical, the CNN differed by up to 0.013 after one epoch."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Experiments: Seeds, Logging & Learning Curves","f":"p8-experiments-seeds-and-curves.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Training loss reaches 0.0002 while validation loss rises from 0.52 to 1.15. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Overfitting: the model is memorising the training set","Underfitting","The learning rate is too low","A bug in the validation set"],"a":0,"w":"The overfitting run here used 2,000 examples and a 2 × 1,024 model."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Experiments: Seeds, Logging & Learning Curves","f":"p8-experiments-seeds-and-curves.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why was the per-step loss noisier at batch 32 than at batch 256?","o":["The learning rate was higher","Small batches contain harder examples","Each step’s loss averages fewer examples, so it varies more from batch to batch (roughly as 1/√batch)","It was a bug in the logging"],"a":2,"w":"Measured standard deviation 0.172 vs 0.053, a ratio of 3.2 against √8 ≈ 2.8."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Experiments: Seeds, Logging & Learning Curves","f":"p8-experiments-seeds-and-curves.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which data should choose the training epoch and the learning rate?","o":["The test set","The validation set","The training set","Whichever gives the highest number"],"a":1,"w":"The test set is used once, at the end, so its number stays an unbiased estimate."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Deep Learning Is","f":"01-what-deep-learning-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does “end-to-end” training of a deep network mean?","o":["Training each layer separately, then joining them","Optimising every layer together with one loss, so the early layers learn features that help the output","Training on the whole dataset in one batch","Using the same model for training and serving"],"a":1,"w":"Gradients of the final loss reach the first layer, which is how the features are learned."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Deep Learning Is","f":"01-what-deep-learning-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On CIFAR-10 pixels, an MLP scored 53.8% and gradient boosting 55.7%, but a CNN 90.4%. What explains it?","o":["The CNN had more parameters","MLPs cannot be trained on images","The CNN was trained longer","The CNN’s structure (local filters shared across positions) matches images; the MLP and boosting see 3,072 unrelated inputs"],"a":3,"w":"The MLP had 3.7 million parameters, the CNN 0.56 million."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Deep Learning Is","f":"01-what-deep-learning-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A linear probe on a trained network’s final features scores 89%, against 36% on raw pixels. What does that show?","o":["The network has transformed the input into a space where the classes are nearly linearly separable","The probe overfits","Pixels contain no information about the class","The network memorised the test set"],"a":0,"w":"The jump was largest in the middle of the network (60.5% → 86.5%)."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Deep Learning Is","f":"01-what-deep-learning-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"For a table of 40 hand-chosen numeric columns, what is the best first model to try?","o":["A large transformer","A CNN","Gradient boosting","A one-layer perceptron"],"a":2,"w":"It won both tables measured here, faster and with less tuning."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Deep Learning Is","f":"01-what-deep-learning-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With only 1,000 CIFAR-10 images, how did the CNN compare with boosting trained on 50,000?","o":["Much worse: deep learning always needs more data","Better: 60.2% against 55.7%, because its built-in assumptions replace much of the data","Exactly the same","It could not be trained"],"a":1,"w":"A fitting architecture is worth a great deal of data."},{"n":2,"ti":"The Neuron & the Perceptron","f":"02-the-neuron-and-the-perceptron.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The perceptron misclassifies an example x with label y = +1. What does its rule do?","o":["Subtracts x from the weights","Halves the weights","Adds x to the weights, which raises that example’s score","Computes a gradient and steps against it"],"a":2,"w":"w ← w + yx; the score y(w · x) rises by |x|²."},{"n":2,"ti":"The Neuron & the Perceptron","f":"02-the-neuron-and-the-perceptron.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can a single neuron never compute XOR?","o":["XOR is not linearly separable, and one neuron draws one hyperplane","It needs more training epochs","The bias is missing","The learning rate is too high"],"a":0,"w":"The best single line gets three of four points right."},{"n":2,"ti":"The Neuron & the Perceptron","f":"02-the-neuron-and-the-perceptron.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can’t gradient descent train a network of step-activation neurons?","o":["Steps are too slow to compute","Steps only output negative numbers","Gradient descent needs integer weights","The step’s derivative is zero almost everywhere, so gradients carry no information"],"a":3,"w":"The sigmoid, a smooth step, has a positive derivative everywhere."},{"n":2,"ti":"The Neuron & the Perceptron","f":"02-the-neuron-and-the-perceptron.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On Fashion-MNIST the perceptron’s accuracy jumped between 77% and 81% across epochs. What fixed it?","o":["Training for more epochs","Averaging the weights over all steps (84.5%)","A larger learning rate","Removing the bias"],"a":1,"w":"The data are not separable, so the last weights depend on the last examples; the average is stable."},{"n":2,"ti":"The Neuron & the Perceptron","f":"02-the-neuron-and-the-perceptron.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A 2-2-1 sigmoid network trained on XOR from 20 random starts solved it 15 times. What happened in the other 5?","o":["The gradients exploded","XOR cannot be solved with two hidden units","They stopped in a local minimum, three points right, loss 0.348","They overfitted"],"a":2,"w":"Gradient descent finds a minimum, not necessarily the best one; wider layers make bad ones rarer."},{"n":3,"ti":"Autograd from Scratch: a Scalar Engine","f":"03-autograd-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In c = a × b, what gradient does the backward rule send to a?","o":["c.grad","b × c.grad","a × c.grad","c.grad / b"],"a":1,"w":"∂c/∂a = b, so a receives the other factor times the upstream gradient."},{"n":3,"ti":"Autograd from Scratch: a Scalar Engine","f":"03-autograd-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does the backward pass visit nodes in reverse topological order?","o":["It is faster than any other order","To use less memory","Because the inputs must be processed first","So every node has received the gradient from all its uses before it passes any on"],"a":3,"w":"A node used twice (like a in the example) must collect both contributions first."},{"n":3,"ti":"Autograd from Scratch: a Scalar Engine","f":"03-autograd-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A backward rule uses self.grad = … instead of self.grad += …. What happens for y = x · x at x = 3?","o":["The gradient comes out as 3 instead of 6: one contribution overwrites the other","It raises an error","The gradient is still 6","The gradient is 9"],"a":0,"w":"Measured exactly: 3.0 against 6.0, with no error."},{"n":3,"ti":"Autograd from Scratch: a Scalar Engine","f":"03-autograd-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the seen set in the topological sort prevent?","o":["Cycles in the graph","Gradients becoming NaN","Visiting shared nodes once per path, which grows exponentially: 2,097,151 visits for 21 nodes here","Forgetting the input nodes"],"a":2,"w":"With it, each node is visited once."},{"n":3,"ti":"Autograd from Scratch: a Scalar Engine","f":"03-autograd-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The scalar engine trained the same model as PyTorch to the same accuracy but 2,479 times more slowly. Why?","o":["Its gradients were less accurate","It builds one Python object per scalar operation (130,618 per step); PyTorch runs each layer as one tensor operation","It used float64","It does not use a topological sort"],"a":1,"w":"Same algorithm, different granularity: Chapter 4 moves to tensors."},{"n":4,"ti":"Backpropagation on Tensors","f":"04-backpropagation-on-tensors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"z = x Wᵀ + b with x (B, in) and W (out, in). Given dz (B, out), what is dW?","o":["xᵀ dz","dz W","dzᵀ x","dz xᵀ"],"a":2,"w":"(out, B) @ (B, in) = (out, in), W’s shape. xᵀdz would be its transpose."},{"n":4,"ti":"Backpropagation on Tensors","f":"04-backpropagation-on-tensors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the backward pass of ReLU do to the incoming gradient?","o":["Passes it through where the forward input was positive and zeroes it elsewhere","Multiplies it by the output","Takes its absolute value","Divides it by the batch size"],"a":0,"w":"Its Jacobian is diagonal with entries 0 or 1: a mask kept from the forward pass."},{"n":4,"ti":"Backpropagation on Tensors","f":"04-backpropagation-on-tensors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A backward pass averages the bias gradient over the batch instead of summing it. What did that do here?","o":["Training diverged","PyTorch raised an error","Accuracy fell to chance","Training looked normal (86.9% vs 87.1%) but the gradient check failed with a relative error of 0.984"],"a":3,"w":"The most dangerous bugs still train. Gradient checks catch them."},{"n":4,"ti":"Backpropagation on Tensors","f":"04-backpropagation-on-tensors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In float64, a hand-written gradient differs from autograd by a relative error of 2 × 10⁻¹⁶. What does that mean?","o":["There is a small bug","It is correct: that is rounding error","The step size h is too small","The batch is too small to tell"],"a":1,"w":"Below about 10⁻⁷ in float64 is correct; above 10⁻³ is a bug."},{"n":4,"ti":"Backpropagation on Tensors","f":"04-backpropagation-on-tensors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does a training step cost about three forward passes?","o":["Backward runs the forward pass twice more","The optimiser step is as expensive as a forward pass","Each linear layer’s backward needs two matrix multiplies (dW and dx) of the forward’s size","Autograd adds overhead equal to two forward passes"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: 15.9 ms forward, 22.0 ms backward on a deep 4,096-wide MLP."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multilayer Perceptrons","f":"05-multilayer-perceptrons.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How many parameters does nn.Linear(256, 10) have?","o":["2,560","266","2,570","2,816"],"a":2,"w":"256 × 10 weights plus 10 biases."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multilayer Perceptrons","f":"05-multilayer-perceptrons.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A one-hidden-layer ReLU network with 16 units maps a single input x to a single output. What shape is its function?","o":["Piecewise linear, with at most 16 kinks","A smooth polynomial of degree 16","A straight line","A step function with 16 steps"],"a":0,"w":"Each unit is a hinge at x = −b/w; the output is a weighted sum of hinges."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multilayer Perceptrons","f":"05-multilayer-perceptrons.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A 5-layer network of width 2 can represent a 32-tooth sawtooth exactly. What happened when networks were trained on it?","o":["The deep networks found it easily","Only the exact architecture found it","Wide shallow networks fitted it perfectly","None got close: all scored near the error of predicting the mean"],"a":3,"w":"Representable is not learnable: gradient descent from random weights rarely finds such solutions."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multilayer Perceptrons","f":"05-multilayer-perceptrons.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On Fashion-MNIST, what did going from 1 to 4 hidden layers do at width 512?","o":["Added about 5 points","Added about 0.35 points, within the seed spread","Lost 3 points","Doubled training speed"],"a":1,"w":"88.26% → 88.61%. Width 32 → 512 was worth about 2 points."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multilayer Perceptrons","f":"05-multilayer-perceptrons.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is a ReLU network’s decision boundary made of straight segments?","o":["Because the output layer is linear","Because training stops early","Within each region where the same units are active, the network is a linear function","It is not; the boundaries are smooth curves"],"a":2,"w":"Each ReLU switches along a hyperplane, so the input space is cut into linear regions."},{"n":6,"ti":"Activation Functions","f":"06-activation-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the largest possible derivative of the sigmoid?","o":["1","0.25, at z = 0","0.5","It is unbounded"],"a":1,"w":"σ′ = σ(1 − σ), largest when σ = 0.5. Ten such factors multiply to at most about 10⁻⁶."},{"n":6,"ti":"Activation Functions","f":"06-activation-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What makes a ReLU unit “dead”?","o":["Its weights become NaN","Its output is always positive","It has a zero bias","Its input is negative for every example, so it outputs 0 and receives zero gradient forever"],"a":3,"w":"No gradient means no update, so it cannot recover on its own."},{"n":6,"ti":"Activation Functions","f":"06-activation-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does Leaky ReLU avoid dead units?","o":["Its derivative for negative inputs is small but not zero, so every unit always receives some gradient","It is bounded","It is smooth everywhere","It uses a larger learning rate"],"a":0,"w":"A 0.01 slope keeps a gradient flowing. Measured here, though, it did not rescue a learning rate that was far too high."},{"n":6,"ti":"Activation Functions","f":"06-activation-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which activation should the final layer of a classifier trained with F.cross_entropy use?","o":["Softmax","Sigmoid","None: output logits and let the loss apply log-softmax","ReLU"],"a":2,"w":"Applying softmax first is the double-softmax bug from the course home page."},{"n":6,"ti":"Activation Functions","f":"06-activation-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Where are GELU and SiLU most commonly used?","o":["Output layers of regression models","The MLP blocks of transformers (GELU in BERT and GPT, SiLU in SwiGLU)","LSTM gates","Only in small networks"],"a":1,"w":"They behave like ReLU but are smooth and never have a zero-gradient region."},{"n":7,"ti":"Loss Functions for Neural Networks","f":"07-losses-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"An image can contain any number of 20 possible objects. Which loss fits?","o":["Softmax cross-entropy over the 20 classes","Binary cross-entropy with logits, one output per object","MSE on one-hot vectors","Focal loss with a softmax"],"a":1,"w":"Each label is an independent yes/no question, and a sigmoid’s probability means the same whatever the count."},{"n":7,"ti":"Loss Functions for Neural Networks","f":"07-losses-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Label smoothing of 0.2 raised accuracy slightly. What happened to calibration in this chapter’s measurement?","o":["It improved: ECE fell","Unchanged","It got much worse: the model became under-confident, ECE 3.2% → 14.7%","The model became over-confident"],"a":2,"w":"Predictions given ~0.77 confidence were right ~96% of the time."},{"n":7,"ti":"Loss Functions for Neural Networks","f":"07-losses-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Class weights raised a rare class’s recall from 13% to 47%, but its average precision did not change. What does that suggest?","o":["The unweighted model already ranked the class well; mostly the decision threshold moved","The weights had no effect","The model overfitted","Average precision was computed wrongly"],"a":0,"w":"AP is threshold-free; recall at one threshold is not."},{"n":7,"ti":"Loss Functions for Neural Networks","f":"07-losses-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did 5% of corrupted targets hurt the MSE model so much more than the Huber model?","o":["Huber uses a larger learning rate","MSE cannot be minimised with Adam","Huber ignores all large targets","MSE’s gradient grows with the residual, so outliers pull hardest; Huber’s is capped beyond δ"],"a":3,"w":"Test RMSE 1.38 (MSE) against 0.53 (Huber) when trained on corrupted prices."},{"n":7,"ti":"Loss Functions for Neural Networks","f":"07-losses-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What problem was focal loss designed for?","o":["Regression with outliers","Dense object detection, where easy background examples vastly outnumber objects and swamp the gradient","Multi-label classification","Calibrating probabilities"],"a":1,"w":"It down-weights well-classified examples; with one rare class among ten it did not help here."},{"n":8,"ti":"Stochastic Gradient Descent & Mini-Batches","f":"08-sgd-and-mini-batches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Quadrupling the batch size does what to the error of the gradient estimate?","o":["Divides it by four","Nothing","Halves it, since the noise falls as 1/√B","Doubles it"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: 3.88, 1.93, 1.01, 0.52, 0.24, 0.13 for batches 8 to 8,192."},{"n":8,"ti":"Stochastic Gradient Descent & Mini-Batches","f":"08-sgd-and-mini-batches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"For the same five epochs, why did batch 8 beat the full batch by 23 points?","o":["It took 37,500 steps against 5","Small batches have more accurate gradients","The full batch used a worse learning rate","Small batches regularise the model"],"a":0,"w":"Progress is made per step; many noisy steps beat a few exact ones."},{"n":8,"ti":"Stochastic Gradient Descent & Mini-Batches","f":"08-sgd-and-mini-batches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On f = ½λx², gradient descent with learning rate lr converges when…","o":["lr < 1/λ only","lr > 2/λ","Always, given enough steps","lr < 2/λ"],"a":3,"w":"Each step multiplies the distance by 1 − lr·λ; between 1/λ and 2/λ it oscillates but still converges."},{"n":8,"ti":"Stochastic Gradient Descent & Mini-Batches","f":"08-sgd-and-mini-batches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does a high condition number mean for gradient descent?","o":["The loss is convex","Steep and flat directions coexist, so a rate safe for the steep one crawls along the flat one","The gradient is zero","Larger batches are needed"],"a":1,"w":"On the ravine (curvatures 1 and 20), lr 0.02 left f = 0.71 after 60 steps."},{"n":8,"ti":"Stochastic Gradient Descent & Mini-Batches","f":"08-sgd-and-mini-batches.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The best learning rate here rose from 0.01 at batch 8 to 0.3 at batch 1,024. Why?","o":["Bigger batches have smaller gradients","It is a coincidence","Less noisy gradients tolerate bigger steps before the noise causes trouble","Larger batches always need lr = 0.3"],"a":2,"w":"The relationship is formalised as the linear scaling rule in Chapter 24."},{"n":9,"ti":"Weight Initialisation","f":"09-weight-initialisation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can’t all weights start at the same value?","o":["It causes overflow","Every unit in a layer gets the same gradient and stays identical, so the layer acts like one unit","PyTorch forbids it","It makes the learning rate too small"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 1 distinct row out of 256 after three epochs."},{"n":9,"ti":"Weight Initialisation","f":"09-weight-initialisation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A layer has 512 inputs of variance 1 and weights of variance 0.01. What is the variance of each output (before the activation)?","o":["0.01","1","5.12","512"],"a":2,"w":"n · Var(w) · Var(x) = 512 × 0.01 × 1."},{"n":9,"ti":"Weight Initialisation","f":"09-weight-initialisation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Xavier initialisation in a 50-layer ReLU network: what happens to the activations?","o":["They shrink by about half in variance per layer, to ~10⁻⁷ standard deviation by layer 50","They stay at 1","They overflow","They saturate at ±1"],"a":0,"w":"ReLU halves the second moment; Xavier does not compensate. Kaiming does."},{"n":9,"ti":"Weight Initialisation","f":"09-weight-initialisation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did a 20-layer plain ReLU network with PyTorch’s default initialisation stay at chance?","o":["The learning rate was too high","The default weights are too large","PyTorch has a bug","Its weight variance is six times smaller than Kaiming’s, so the input signal faded to a bias-driven constant and the first layers got no gradient"],"a":3,"w":"The first layer’s gradient at init measured 2 × 10⁻¹⁹. With normalisation layers the default is fine."},{"n":9,"ti":"Weight Initialisation","f":"09-weight-initialisation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the quickest check that a new deep network is initialised sensibly?","o":["Train it for an epoch and look at accuracy","One forward pass printing each layer’s activation std, and one backward pass printing each layer’s gradient norm","Count the parameters","Check that the loss is positive"],"a":1,"w":"Shrinking, exploding or wildly uneven numbers point straight at the initial scale."},{"n":10,"ti":"Training an MLP End to End","f":"10-training-an-mlp-end-to-end.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model trained on one batch with shuffled labels drives the loss to almost zero. What does that tell you?","o":["The labels are correct","The model has a bug","Only that the model can fit 64 pairs: the overfit test checks wiring and capacity, not the data","The learning rate is too high"],"a":2,"w":"It reached 0.00003 with shuffled labels, exactly as with correct ones."},{"n":10,"ti":"Training an MLP End to End","f":"10-training-an-mlp-end-to-end.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With the input accidentally zeroed, the one-batch loss stalled at 2.24. Why that value?","o":["The model can only learn the label frequencies, so the loss stalls at their entropy","It is log(10) by coincidence","The learning rate was zero","Dropout was on"],"a":0,"w":"Without input information the best prediction is the batch’s label distribution."},{"n":10,"ti":"Training an MLP End to End","f":"10-training-an-mlp-end-to-end.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Validation loss rose after epoch 11 while validation accuracy kept rising. What is happening?","o":["A bug in the validation code","Underfitting","The learning rate schedule restarted","The model gets more examples right but grows over-confident on its mistakes, which the loss punishes"],"a":3,"w":"The last epoch scored 90.5% on test, the lowest-loss epoch 88.9%."},{"n":10,"ti":"Training an MLP End to End","f":"10-training-an-mlp-end-to-end.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which class did the MLP find hardest on Fashion-MNIST?","o":["Bags","Shirts (67.9%), mostly confused with T-shirts, pullovers and coats","Trousers","Sneakers"],"a":1,"w":"At 28 × 28 a shirt is barely distinguishable from a T-shirt."},{"n":10,"ti":"Training an MLP End to End","f":"10-training-an-mlp-end-to-end.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why inspect the errors with the highest confidence first?","o":["They are the easiest to fix by retraining","They are rare","They concentrate label noise, ambiguous examples and the model’s blind spots, and take minutes to read","They always mean the model is broken"],"a":2,"w":"190 of 1,108 errors had confidence above 0.9, including some apparently mislabelled images."},{"n":11,"ti":"Momentum, RMSProp & Adam","f":"11-momentum-rmsprop-and-adam.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does momentum help in a ravine?","o":["It raises the learning rate","Gradient components that flip sign across the ravine cancel in the average, while the consistent component along the floor accumulates","It normalises the gradient","It adds noise that escapes local minima"],"a":1,"w":"On Rosenbrock, momentum reached 1.4 × 10⁻⁸ where SGD stopped at 0.195."},{"n":11,"ti":"Momentum, RMSProp & Adam","f":"11-momentum-rmsprop-and-adam.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did AdaGrad escape the saddle point in two steps?","o":["It uses momentum","Its learning rate was the largest","It adds random noise","It divides the tiny escape-direction gradient by its own size, turning it into a full-sized step"],"a":3,"w":"SGD took 90 steps, because its step is proportional to the tiny gradient."},{"n":11,"ti":"Momentum, RMSProp & Adam","f":"11-momentum-rmsprop-and-adam.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does Adam’s bias correction fix?","o":["The moving averages start at zero and underestimate the gradient statistics early on","Weight decay applied through the gradient","Exploding gradients","The choice of learning rate"],"a":0,"w":"Dividing by 1 − βᵗ makes the first step g/|g| instead of the wrong size."},{"n":11,"ti":"Momentum, RMSProp & Adam","f":"11-momentum-rmsprop-and-adam.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why are AdaGrad’s steps unsuitable for long training?","o":["It has no momentum","It needs two buffers","It sums every squared gradient ever seen, so its effective step only shrinks","It diverges on convex problems"],"a":2,"w":"RMSProp replaces the sum with a moving average so the scale tracks recent gradients."},{"n":11,"ti":"Momentum, RMSProp & Adam","f":"11-momentum-rmsprop-and-adam.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"With each optimiser tuned, how did SGD with Nesterov momentum compare with AdamW on the CIFAR-10 CNN?","o":["SGD was 3 points better","Within half a point (89.5% vs 89.9%), inside single-seed noise","AdamW was 5 points better","SGD failed to train"],"a":1,"w":"The often-cited gap is largely about Adam with L2 and poor tuning (Chapter 13)."},{"n":12,"ti":"Learning-Rate Schedules & Warm-up","f":"12-learning-rate-schedules.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did the constant-rate run finish nine points behind the decayed ones?","o":["Its peak rate was lower","It overfitted","Fixed-size noisy steps keep the weights bouncing around the minimum instead of settling","It diverged"],"a":2,"w":"Its training loss stayed at 0.39 against 0.22 for one-cycle."},{"n":12,"ti":"Learning-Rate Schedules & Warm-up","f":"12-learning-rate-schedules.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does the one-cycle schedule do?","o":["Rises from a small rate to the peak over the first part of training, then decays to near zero","Keeps the rate constant for one epoch","Divides the rate by 10 once","Restarts the rate every epoch"],"a":0,"w":"Here: 30% of steps up, the rest down along a cosine; 89.2% at 15 epochs."},{"n":12,"ti":"Learning-Rate Schedules & Warm-up","f":"12-learning-rate-schedules.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In this chapter’s transformer, what made the post-norm model train?","o":["1,000 steps of warm-up at 1e-3","More steps at 1e-3","A larger batch","A lower peak rate (3e-4); the pre-norm block trained at 1e-3 even without warm-up"],"a":3,"w":"Warm-up alone left it at chance; the rate and the block design mattered."},{"n":12,"ti":"Learning-Rate Schedules & Warm-up","f":"12-learning-rate-schedules.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does a learning-rate range test do?","o":["Trains a dozen full runs at different rates","Raises the rate exponentially during one short run and reads off where the loss falls fastest and where it breaks","Measures the gradient norm","Finds the best schedule shape"],"a":1,"w":"Here the smoothed loss bottomed between 0.03 and 0.14."},{"n":12,"ti":"Learning-Rate Schedules & Warm-up","f":"12-learning-rate-schedules.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"At epoch 8 of 15, step decay was ahead of cosine (85.0% vs 75.9%). Why is that not evidence it is better?","o":["One seed is never enough","Accuracy is the wrong metric","Step decay had just made its first drop while cosine was still at high rates; only the end of the budget compares like with like","Cosine had diverged"],"a":2,"w":"At the end cosine finished ahead, 88.3% vs 87.4%."},{"n":13,"ti":"Regularisation: Weight Decay, Dropout & Early Stopping","f":"13-regularisation-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is L2 regularisation inside the loss not equivalent to weight decay under Adam?","o":["Adam ignores the loss","L2 only affects biases","The L2 gradient is divided by Adam’s per-parameter scale, so large-gradient weights are decayed less","They are equivalent"],"a":2,"w":"AdamW applies the decay to the weights directly, outside the adaptive scaling."},{"n":13,"ti":"Regularisation: Weight Decay, Dropout & Early Stopping","f":"13-regularisation-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does dropout do at evaluation time?","o":["Nothing: model.eval() switches it off and the full network is used","Drops the same fraction of units","Doubles every activation","Averages several random passes"],"a":0,"w":"Training scales survivors by 1/(1 − p), so no rescaling is needed at evaluation."},{"n":13,"ti":"Regularisation: Weight Decay, Dropout & Early Stopping","f":"13-regularisation-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"On the memorising MLP, what did dropout 0.8 change most?","o":["Test accuracy, +5 points","Training speed, faster","The weight norm, to zero","Confidence: the final validation loss fell from 1.26 to 0.49, with test accuracy about the same"],"a":3,"w":"It also left training accuracy at 90.9%: the network could no longer memorise."},{"n":13,"ti":"Regularisation: Weight Decay, Dropout & Early Stopping","f":"13-regularisation-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Early stopping on validation loss stopped at epoch 5. Why was that wrong here?","o":["The validation set was too small","Validation loss rose from over-confidence while accuracy kept improving to 85.5% by epoch 100","The learning rate was too high","Early stopping never works"],"a":1,"w":"Stop on the metric you report."},{"n":13,"ti":"Regularisation: Weight Decay, Dropout & Early Stopping","f":"13-regularisation-for-neural-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which helped test accuracy most?","o":["AdamW weight decay 1.0","Dropout 0.5","Training on 50,000 images instead of 5,000 (+3 points)","L2 at 10⁻³"],"a":2,"w":"More data is what regularisation tries to imitate."},{"n":14,"ti":"Normalisation Layers: Batch, Layer & RMS","f":"14-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"For a (B, C, H, W) tensor, which axes does BatchNorm average over?","o":["C, H, W, per example","B, H, W, per channel","Only B","H and W only"],"a":1,"w":"One mean and variance per channel, computed across the batch and positions."},{"n":14,"ti":"Normalisation Layers: Batch, Layer & RMS","f":"14-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did a BatchNorm model fall to 11.2% when evaluated in train mode one image at a time?","o":["Dropout was active","The weights changed","Running statistics are wrong","Each channel was normalised by that single image’s own statistics, subtracting away much of what distinguishes classes"],"a":3,"w":"model.eval() makes it use the running statistics: 88.1%."},{"n":14,"ti":"Normalisation Layers: Batch, Layer & RMS","f":"14-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why do transformers use LayerNorm rather than BatchNorm?","o":["It normalises each token on its own, so it works at any batch size and identically in training and inference","It is the only norm that works with attention","It is much faster","It has no learned parameters"],"a":0,"w":"RMSNorm, which skips the mean, is the modern variant."},{"n":14,"ti":"Normalisation Layers: Batch, Layer & RMS","f":"14-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A 20-layer MLP with default initialisation trained with BatchNorm but not with LayerNorm. What did the measurement show?","o":["LayerNorm had a bug","LayerNorm made the gradients explode","Different images’ activations were nearly identical (cosine 0.87); LayerNorm kept that, BatchNorm removed the shared part (cosine 0.01)","BatchNorm used a higher learning rate"],"a":2,"w":"With Kaiming initialisation and zero biases all of them trained."},{"n":14,"ti":"Normalisation Layers: Batch, Layer & RMS","f":"14-normalisation-layers.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why set bias=False on a convolution followed by BatchNorm?","o":["To save memory only","BatchNorm subtracts each channel’s mean, cancelling any bias, and β provides the offset anyway","Biases cause dead units","PyTorch requires it"],"a":1,"w":"The bias would be a wasted parameter."},{"n":15,"ti":"Residual Connections & Very Deep Networks","f":"15-residual-connections.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A 56-layer plain network has lower training accuracy than a 20-layer one. What is this?","o":["Overfitting","Underfitting caused by too few parameters","The degradation problem: an optimisation failure, since the deeper network could represent the shallower one","A data leak"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: 54.4% vs 89.3% training accuracy; with skips, 93.3%."},{"n":15,"ti":"Residual Connections & Very Deep Networks","f":"15-residual-connections.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"For y = x + f(x), what is ∂y/∂x?","o":["I + ∂f/∂x","∂f/∂x","I","x · ∂f/∂x"],"a":0,"w":"The identity term is the direct path that carries gradients past every block."},{"n":15,"ti":"Residual Connections & Very Deep Networks","f":"15-residual-connections.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"When a block halves the resolution and doubles the channels, what does the shortcut need?","o":["Nothing","A ReLU","To be removed","A projection, such as a 1 × 1 stride-2 convolution, so x has the same shape as f(x)"],"a":3,"w":"You can only add tensors of the same shape."},{"n":15,"ti":"Residual Connections & Very Deep Networks","f":"15-residual-connections.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is the difference between pre-norm and post-norm?","o":["Pre-norm has no normalisation","Pre-norm normalises the branch input, x + f(Norm(x)); post-norm normalises after the addition, Norm(x + f(x))","Post-norm is only used in CNNs","They are the same"],"a":1,"w":"Pre-norm leaves the identity path clean and trains more stably."},{"n":15,"ti":"Residual Connections & Very Deep Networks","f":"15-residual-connections.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"At 8 layers, plain and residual networks were indistinguishable. Why?","o":["Residuals only work with transformers","The 8-layer residual net was broken","A short plain stack is still easy to optimise; the skip matters as depth makes the product of Jacobians unstable","8 layers is too few to use BatchNorm"],"a":2,"w":"89.0% training accuracy for both; the gap opened at 20 layers and became a collapse at 56."},{"n":16,"ti":"Convolutional Networks: the Core Idea","f":"16-convolutional-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How many weights (excluding biases) does nn.Conv2d(64, 128, 3) have?","o":["576","73,728","8,192","It depends on the image size"],"a":1,"w":"128 × 64 × 3 × 3, independent of H and W."},{"n":16,"ti":"Convolutional Networks: the Core Idea","f":"16-convolutional-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A 32 × 32 input goes through a 3 × 3 convolution with padding 1 and stride 2. What is the output size?","o":["32 × 32","15 × 15","16 × 16","30 × 30"],"a":2,"w":"⌊(32 + 2 − 3) / 2⌋ + 1 = 16."},{"n":16,"ti":"Convolutional Networks: the Core Idea","f":"16-convolutional-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did an MLP fall to chance when Fashion-MNIST images were shifted 6 pixels?","o":["Each of its weights is tied to one pixel position, so a pattern in a new place hits different weights","It was undertrained","It had fewer parameters than the CNN","The shift changed the labels"],"a":0,"w":"The CNN’s shared filters respond to the pattern wherever it is: 66.7%."},{"n":16,"ti":"Convolutional Networks: the Core Idea","f":"16-convolutional-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does im2col do?","o":["Compresses images","Converts colour to greyscale","Computes a Fourier transform","Unfolds every input patch into a column so the convolution becomes one matrix multiply"],"a":3,"w":"Here it turned a 16 MB input into 144 MB of patches, which is why libraries avoid materialising it."},{"n":16,"ti":"Convolutional Networks: the Core Idea","f":"16-convolutional-networks.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two stacked 3 × 3 convolutions (stride 1) have what receptive field?","o":["3 × 3","5 × 5","6 × 6","9 × 9"],"a":1,"w":"Each adds k − 1 = 2 pixels; with fewer weights than one 5 × 5 layer and an extra non-linearity."},{"n":17,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & Backprop Through Time","f":"17-recurrent-networks-and-bptt.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How is the gradient of a shared recurrent weight computed in BPTT?","o":["From the last step only","As the average of the first and last steps","As the sum of its gradients from every time step of the unrolled graph","It is not trained"],"a":2,"w":"The same weight appears at every step, so its contributions add up."},{"n":17,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & Backprop Through Time","f":"17-recurrent-networks-and-bptt.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A recurrent matrix has spectral radius 0.9. Roughly what fraction of the gradient survives 100 steps of a linear recurrence?","o":["About 10⁻⁵ (measured 3.4 × 10⁻⁵)","About 0.9","About 0.1","All of it"],"a":0,"w":"0.9¹⁰⁰ ≈ 2.7 × 10⁻⁵."},{"n":17,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & Backprop Through Time","f":"17-recurrent-networks-and-bptt.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does gradient clipping do?","o":["Sets small gradients to zero","Rounds gradients to fp16","Clips each weight to [−1, 1]","Rescales the gradient vector when its norm exceeds a threshold, keeping its direction"],"a":3,"w":"It turned a run stuck at loss 2.10 into one that reached 0.0023."},{"n":17,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & Backprop Through Time","f":"17-recurrent-networks-and-bptt.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did an LSTM with forget-gate bias 1 solve the 30-step memory task when the default LSTM did not?","o":["It had more parameters","The forget gate starts nearer 1, so the cell state and its gradient pass through each step largely intact","It used a larger learning rate","It was trained longer"],"a":1,"w":"With the gate at about 0.5 the cell state halves each step: 0.5³⁰ ≈ 10⁻⁹."},{"n":17,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & Backprop Through Time","f":"17-recurrent-networks-and-bptt.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In truncated BPTT, what does h.detach() at a window boundary do?","o":["Resets the hidden state to zero","Deletes the model’s memory","Keeps the hidden state’s value for the next window but stops gradients flowing back past the boundary","Moves the state to the CPU"],"a":2,"w":"Memory stays proportional to the window length k."}];
